   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "definition",
     "title": "Vectors",
     "md": "A vector has magnitude and direction. In component form $\\vec{a}=x\\text{i}+y\\text{j}$, written here as the pair $(x,y)$. Magnitude $|\\vec a|=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$; a unit vector is $\\frac{\\vec a}{|\\vec a|}$. The position vector of $A$ is $\\vec{OA}$ and $\\vec{AB}=\\vec{OB}-\\vec{OA}$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "propriete",
     "title": "Parallel and collinear",
     "md": "$\\vec a$ and $\\vec b$ are parallel if $\\vec a=\\lambda\\vec b$. Points $A,B,C$ are collinear if $\\vec{AB}$ is parallel to $\\vec{BC}$. A point $P$ dividing $AB$ in the ratio $m:n$ has $\\vec{OP}=\\frac{n\\vec{OA}+m\\vec{OB}}{m+n}$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "formule",
     "title": "Scalar (dot) product",
     "md": "$\\vec a\\cdot\\vec b=x_1x_2+y_1y_2=|\\vec a||\\vec b|\\cos\\theta$, where $\\theta$ is the angle between them. Hence $\\cos\\theta=\\frac{\\vec a\\cdot\\vec b}{|\\vec a||\\vec b|}$. Vectors are perpendicular iff $\\vec a\\cdot\\vec b=0$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "The scalar product is a NUMBER, not a vector. $\\vec{AB}=\\vec{OB}-\\vec{OA}$ (end minus start). The magnitude of $\\vec{AB}$ is a length, never negative."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
